"/bin/zsh -lc \"codex exec --sandbox read-only --ephemeral --skip-git-repo-check --output-schema /dev/stdin 'Perform independent read-only review of this setup plan. Do not write files or call mutating tools. Setup proposal for Neighbourhood Tool Library, empty repository. Source: conversation. Two volunteer librarians, about 80 members, 120 tools in spreadsheet (name/category/condition). Members see availability only; librarians add tools, lend to a member with due date, record returns, see overdue. One shared laptop, browser, no member data leaves building. Reservations and email reminders explicitly deferred. No stack preference.\nArchitecture proposal, not chosen technology: local application served only on loopback, durable on-laptop database outside cloud-synced directories; no hosted services, telemetry, CDN runtime resources, or member data in git/AI prompts. Backup media stays in building. Type generic/language TBD at setup; implementation stack, OS/startup, backup destination, member identifier and librarian credential recovery remain owner decisions in A. Member access assumed on same laptop, not remote; confirm in A. No actual personal data needed for setup.\nMilestones:\nM1 agree workflow and local operating plan; A: workflow and feasibility specification; B: early artifact checkpoint.\nM2 working catalogue and lending; C-G implementation slices.\nM3 recoverable usable release; H backups, I operational/privacy verification, J acceptance checkpoint.\n\nAll implementation tickets use this template:\nA Local operating specification and screen sketch. Outcome: clarify local-only user journey from conversation. Scope: options/tradeoffs for stack, laptop OS/startup, on-site member browsing, member identifier needed to disambiguate, librarian sign-in/lock/recovery, backup medium/location, spreadsheet format export. Excludes remote access/reservations/email. Acceptance: concrete decisions and sample availability, checkout, overdue screens using fictional data; 